    },
    {
      "id": "08-COHOMOLOGY.md#002",
      "path": [
        "08-COHOMOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:topology:08-cohomology, git-history:topology:08-cohomology]",
        "canonical_path": "topology/08-COHOMOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[cohomology]",
        "current_path": "topology/08-COHOMOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:topology:cohomology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "topology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[cohomology]",
        "section": "topology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Cohomology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+====================================================================+\n|        COHOMOLOGY — THE DUAL THEORY WITH RING STRUCTURE            |\n+====================================================================+\n|                                                                    |\n|  HOMOLOGY: chains (add up geometric pieces) → groups Hₙ(X).      |\n|  COHOMOLOGY: cochains (assign numbers to pieces) → groups Hⁿ(X). |\n|                                                                    |\n|  RELATIONSHIP: Hⁿ(X;Z) ≅ Hom(Hₙ(X),Z) ⊕ Ext(Hₙ₋₁(X),Z)        |\n|  (Universal Coefficient Theorem — cohomology ≈ dual of homology)  |\n|                                                                    |\n|  WHY COHOMOLOGY IS RICHER:                                        |\n|  Cohomology groups Hⁿ(X;R) form a graded RING under cup product: |\n|  ⌣: Hᵖ(X) ⊗ Hq(X) → Hᵖ⁺q(X)                                    |\n|  This ring structure distinguishes spaces with the same groups.   |\n|                                                                    |\n|  DE RHAM COHOMOLOGY (for smooth manifolds):                       |\n|  HᵈᴿⁿR(M) = {closed n-forms} / {exact n-forms}                   |\n|  De Rham's theorem: HᵈᴿⁿR(M;R) ≅ Hⁿ(M;R).                       |\n|  Bridges topology, analysis, and differential geometry.           |\n+====================================================================+\n```"
    },
